            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Pick</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Folded size (listing)</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Fold type</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Running area</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">THERUN Manual Incline Treadmill</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">36.7 x 26.3 x 48.6 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Foot-bar release, self-lowering</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">16.5 x 43.3 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">THERUN 16% Folding Incline Treadmill</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">29.5 x 31.1 x 54.7 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hydraulic</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">17 x 47.2 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">MERACH 18% Auto Incline Treadmill</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hydraulic</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">18 x 47 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Schwinn 810</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">SoftDrop</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 55 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack Commercial LE</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Foot-bar assisted lowering</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack T Series 16</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Foot-bar assisted lowering</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Where a listing gives no folded size, ask the seller or download the owner’s manual
            before you order; manuals usually include a dimensioned drawing. Then plan for three
            spaces, not one. The folded footprint is where it lives. The working footprint is the
            unfolded length and width plus clearance: treadmill makers commonly recommend open
            space behind the deck and at the sides, and your manual gives the figure for your model.
            And the ceiling height matters on an incline machine, because the deck rises at the front
            and you rise with it. Add the deck height at full incline to your own height and leave
            room for the bounce of a jog. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-small-spaces/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for small spaces</a> guide walks through the measurements in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="powered-or-manual" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Powered or Manual Incline on a Folding Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Five of the six picks have powered incline, and for most people that is worth the extra
            money. A motor lets you change the grade mid-walk with a button, follow interval
            programs that raise and lower the deck, and on the NordicTrack machines let an app run
            the hill for you. That is how most people actually use incline: a few minutes up, a few
            minutes down, rather than one grade for the whole session.
          </p>
          <p>
            Manual incline still has a place. It is far cheaper, as the $220 THERUN shows, and it has
            no incline motor to wear out or fault. If your plan is a daily walk at a fixed gentle
            grade, set once before you step on, manual incline does exactly that. What it cannot do
            is intervals, and you will not change it often in practice because each change means
            stopping, stepping off and moving the deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            A practical test: if you can picture yourself changing the incline more than once in a
            typical session, buy powered. If you can picture yourself setting it to 4% and leaving it
            there for months, manual will do, and the saving buys a better pair of shoes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-much-incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Much Incline Do You Need on a Folding Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Less than the listings suggest. Treadmill incline is given as a percentage of rise over
            distance, which always sounds steeper than the same slope in degrees.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Grade</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">About</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What it suits</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Picks that reach it</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1-3%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.6-1.7°</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Easy runs, offsetting the flat belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">All six</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.4°</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Steady brisk walks</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">All six</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5.7°</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hard incline walking, hill repeats</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">All except the manual THERUN</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6.8°</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Popular incline walking workouts</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Both NordicTracks, THERUN 16%, MERACH</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">16-18%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">9.1-10.2°</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hiking practice, steep climbs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">THERUN 16% (16%), MERACH (18%)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Most home users spend most of their time between 3% and 10%. A 12% ceiling covers the
            well-known 12% incline <a href="/treadmill-walking-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking workouts</a>, and 15% or more matters mainly to hikers training
            for steep trails or to walkers who want more effort without walking faster. If that is
            you, the THERUN 16% and the MERACH are the folding options; if you want steeper still,
            that is incline-trainer territory, and those do not fold.
          </p>
          <p>
            Steep incline walking is hard work, so build up over weeks rather than starting at the
            top of the range. If you have a heart, joint or balance condition, ask your doctor or
            physical therapist what grade and duration suit you; this site reviews equipment and is
            not giving medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decline-question" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Do You Need Decline on a Folding Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Probably not, but if you do, the NordicTrack Commercial LE is the folding route. Decline
            tilts the front of the deck below level so you walk or run downhill, which matters for
            runners preparing for downhill courses and hikers preparing for long descents. It is
            otherwise a feature most owners try a few times and forget.
          </p>
          <p>
            The useful point for this page is that decline does not cost you the fold. The
            Commercial LE goes from -3% to 12%, folds with assisted lowering, and costs the same on
            Amazon as the <a href="/nordictrack-t-series-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>T Series 16</a>, which climbs to 12% but does not go below zero. If you are
            choosing between those two, the decision is decline or a built-in screen. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills with decline</a> guide covers the steeper -6% machines, which cost considerably more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding-safely" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Folding an Incline Treadmill Safely</h2>
<p>
            Incline adds two steps to the usual folding routine. Follow your manual first; these are
            the general points that apply to most machines.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Lower the incline before you fold.</strong> Some manuals ask you to return the deck to its lowest grade and stop the belt before raising it. A deck folded at an angle can sit unevenly and strain the lift mechanism.</li>
            <li><strong>Switch off and unplug.</strong> Folding with the power on risks an accidental start, and a cord stretched across the fold is a trip hazard.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the latch.</strong> Make sure the deck locks upright before you let go, and keep children and pets clear of the hinge and the space the deck swings through.</li>
            <li><strong>Lower it slowly.</strong> Even with a hydraulic or soft-drop fold, stand clear of the deck’s path and guide it rather than letting it fall on its own.</li>
            <li><strong>Roll on hard floors.</strong> Transport wheels are small; move the machine over carpet edges and door thresholds with care, and lock or rest it level before use.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Skip a Folding Incline Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Three groups are better served elsewhere. <strong>Heavier users</strong> should look at the
            listed capacity carefully: the Schwinn 810 stops at 275 lb, and the two NordicTracks do
            not state one on Amazon. A <a href="/best-non-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>non-folding treadmill</a> with a higher rated capacity is usually
            sturdier for the same money. <strong>Serious runners</strong> who run fast and long will
            find 47-inch decks short, and even 55 inches is snug at speed for tall runners; a
            non-folding runner’s treadmill with a 60-inch deck is the better tool. <strong>Steep hikers</strong> who want 30% or more need an incline trainer, and those do not
            fold.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if your real constraint is that the machine must slide under a sofa or bed, no
            folding treadmill on this page will do it; that is a walking pad’s job, and incline on
            walking pads is usually a few per cent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes People Make Buying a Folding Treadmill With Incline</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from buying a walking pad by mistake, or measuring for the folded machine and forgetting the one you will actually use.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a walking pad by accident</h3>
              <p>Search results for folding incline treadmills are mostly walking pads with fold-down handles. Check for a console, side rails, a stated deck length of at least 43 inches and an incline given as a percentage.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Measuring only the folded size</h3>
              <p>The machine spends your workouts unfolded and tilted. Measure the unfolded length plus the clearance your manual asks for, and your ceiling height with the deck at full incline.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Missing the version on a multi-version listing</h3>
              <p>Some listings cover two models with different inclines, belts and speeds in one title. Read the bullets and check the version selected in your basket before you pay.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for 18% and using 4%</h3>
              <p>The highest grade on a listing is not the one you will use most. If your plan is steady walks at 3% to 6%, a sturdier, longer deck matters more than a steep ceiling.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            For most people who want a treadmill that climbs and then folds away, the <strong class="text-white">Schwinn 810</strong> is the one to buy: a 10% motorized incline,
            a 20 by 55-inch running path and a soft-drop fold for about $584. If you want hills in both
            directions, the <strong class="text-white">NordicTrack Commercial LE</strong> adds -3%
            decline to a 12% climb and still folds.
          </p>
          <p>
            If steepness matters more than deck length, the <strong class="text-white">THERUN 16%</strong> and the <strong class="text-white">MERACH</strong> reach 16% and 18% on folding frames for
            under $500, as walking machines. Whichever you choose, measure the folded size, the
            working space and the ceiling at full incline before you order, and lower the deck before
            you fold it.
          </p>
        </div>

