        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best folding treadmill with incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people, the Schwinn 810: a 10% motorized incline, a 20 by 55-inch running path, SoftDrop folding and a 10-year motor and frame warranty per its Amazon listing, for about $584 on 21 November 2026. If you need decline, the NordicTrack Commercial LE folds and runs from -3% to 12%.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best folding treadmill for a small space with incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Choose one that states its folded size. Of our picks, the THERUN 16% model folds to 29.5 x 31.1 x 54.7 inches and the THERUN manual-incline model to 36.7 x 26.3 x 48.6 inches, per their listings. Measure the folded footprint, the unfolded footprint with clearance, and your ceiling height at full incline.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which folding treadmill has the highest incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Among the folding treadmills we shortlisted on 21 November 2026, the MERACH listing describes the steepest grade, 18% auto incline, on one of its two versions. The THERUN 16% model reaches 16%. Branded full-size folding machines from Schwinn and NordicTrack stop at 10% to 12%.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a folding treadmill with incline as sturdy as a non-folding one?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually not quite, at the same price, because the hinge and lighter frame add flex. Good folding models are perfectly stable for walking and normal running. If you are near a machine’s weight limit or run hard every day, a non-folding treadmill is the safer choice.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you fold a treadmill with the incline raised?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Check your manual; some ask you to lower the deck to its lowest incline and stop the belt before folding. Folding at an angle can leave the deck sitting unevenly and put strain on the lift mechanism.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is manual incline worth it on a folding treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if you will set one grade per session and leave it. Manual incline is cheaper and has no motor to fail, but changing it means stopping and stepping off. If you want hill intervals, buy powered incline.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need an iFIT membership to use incline on a NordicTrack?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. You can set speed and incline from the console yourself. Per the Amazon listings, the membership adds workouts that change speed and incline for you, heart-rate control and app syncing; on the T Series 16 that is iFIT Pro at $39 a month.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

